title mycelium Lapse r1 — a reservation the order lets expire on time, decided once by the clock
prompt loop card: take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; approve your own design rounds and make progress; send often as its own signed round
think r1 of the Lapse arc opened by this session's design read. The crux: a Pledge that carries a deadline measured in the agreed order's own position — never the wall, which two nodes never share — and the ledger itself lapses it when the order's clock reaches that position, returning reserved coins to the pledger with no further signature. This is TigerBeetle's third signature primitive (pending-transfer timeout) after the two-phase Pledge and the linked Braid.
think Design: the deadline rides inside the signed pledge body (P · id(16) · deadline(8 le u64) · to(32)) so it cannot be moved after the signature. fold_lapse ticks the clock FIRST — lapse_expired(l, pos) returns reserved -> balance for every open pledge whose deadline <= pos, state -> lapsed — THEN applies the fact at pos. So a post the order places at or past the deadline finds the pledge already lapsed and is a lawful no-op. A deadline not strictly after its opening pos refuses BadDeadline (expired at birth). Conservation is the Pledge's law unwidened: a lapse is a void by another cause (reserved -> balance), so balances + reserved == issued on every path.
think The determinism crux is proven the way Pledge/Braid proved theirs: the late scene seats post AND void as siblings on the pledge block (round 2, positions 2 and 3); the deadline (2) lapses the pledge at position 2 before either settles, so both are no-ops, and swapping the two round-2 facts' arrival names the identical ledger (ledgers_equal). Whether an honor is on time is a property of the committed order, not of arrival. Two clean companion facts sharpen the meaning of order-as-clock: the SAME deadline of 2 with the order walking no further (only g + pledge) stays OPEN — position 2 was never reached; the same post under deadline 4 honors. A clean pre-deadline void releases. Refusals: bad-deadline · unknown pledge · wrong signer · amount mismatch · star reserve each refuse whole.
think Modeled closely on pledge.rye (composing cord + fold + kumara public API only, editing none), so the two-phase machinery is a known-correct spine; the one new surface is the deadline field and the lapse_expired clock step. GREEN on the first run; the witness re-runs the Cord and the fold at the door. TAME style bans clean, width-check exit 0, no lapse violations.
obs Named lapse (grep-clean before the design round; fiber/homey kin to Cord · Knot · Till · Purse · Pledge · Braid, Comlink-tendency clear/warm/safe). lapse_expired proves an important honest bound: the reserve is always still held by its pledger when the deadline fires (assert reserved >= amount), and the return-to-balance can never wrap (those coins left this balance when the pledge opened).
loom witness=mycelium_lapse_witness build=ok selftest=GREEN cord=GREEN fold=GREEN
file mycelium/lapse.rye r1 crux — the Lapse: a two-phase transfer with a deadline in the order's own clock; late honor loses to expiry, decided once, conservation unwidened
file tools/mycelium_lapse_witness.rish GREEN witness — proves the order-clock deadline, late-honor-finds-lapsed, arrival independence, conservation, unreached-stays-open, and every refusal; the Cord and fold stand at the door
recommend keep-going Lapse r2 (the verdict travels as a format lapse-v1 record, parse round-trips byte-for-byte, conservation as the record's own law, mirroring pledge_bron/braid_bron), then r3 across a Knot, r4 true to the bytes. A served lapse · per-account mint authority · breach renames (cairn-first) · deep debride · seed force-push stay the maintainer's hand.
